#X text 240 83 A non-interpolating chaotic generator based on the standard
map (also known as the Chirikov–Taylor map) \, a two-dimensional discrete
dynamical system. On each bang the generator advances the map by one
step and outputs the new x and y coordinates \, mapped to the [-1 \,
1] range. The map is widely used as a model of chaos in Hamiltonian
systems.;
#X text 240 199 The map is defined by the difference equations:;
#X text 240 224 y(n+1) = (y(n) + k * sin(x(n))) mod 2π;
#X text 240 249 x(n+1) = (x(n) + y(n+1)) mod 2π;
#X text 240 274 The value of k controls the behavior of the map. For
small k (roughly k < 1) the trajectories are mostly regular (quasi-periodic
or periodic) \, while for larger k the system becomes increasingly
chaotic. The default k = 1 puts the generator near the boundary between
regular and chaotic motion.;
#X text 240 375 Since the values are not interpolated \, driving the
generator with a slow metro produces a stepped chaotic control signal
\, useful for driving filters \, pan positions or pitches.;
#X obj 240 448 ui.link @background_color 0.90196 0.90196 0.90196
@title wiki: Standard map @url
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_map;
#X text 240 469 The chaotic properties of the standard map were established
by Boris Chirikov in 1969\.;
